    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p44-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Express $DE$ in terms of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$DE=\\sqrt{3x^2-6x+4}\\text{ cm}$.",
          "worked": "$BD=2-x$ and $\\angle DBE=60^\\circ$. By the cosine rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}DE^2&=(2-x)^2+x^2-2(2-x)x\\cos60^\\circ\\\\DE^2&=3x^2-6x+4\\\\DE&=\\sqrt{3x^2-6x+4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the area of $\\triangle DEF$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}(3x^2-6x+4)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q10-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle DEF)=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}(3x^2-6x+4)\\text{ cm}^2",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q10-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The inner triangle is equilateral; use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac12DE^2\\sin60^\\circ",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Applying the same cosine-rule calculation to $EF$ and $FD$ gives $DE=EF=FD$. Thus $\\triangle DEF$ is equilateral.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle DEF)&=\\frac12DE^2\\sin60^\\circ\\\\\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle DEF)&=\\frac12(3x^2-6x+4)\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle DEF)&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}(3x^2-6x+4)\\text{ cm}^2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the value of $x$ such that the area of $\\triangle DEF$ is smallest.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q10-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=1\\text{ cm}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q10-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Write ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "3x^2-6x+4=3(x-1)^2+1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " to identify the minimum.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle DEF)&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}(3x^2-6x+4)\\\\\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle DEF)&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\bigl(3(x-1)^2+1\\bigr).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $(x-1)^2\\geq0$, the area is smallest when $x=1\\text{ cm}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
  },
